    "summary": "High-energy set with stellar Looks Like Rain and explosive Playin' jam",
    "blurb": "The show shines on Jerry's pedal‑steel on Looks Like Rain, a smoking Brokedown Palace, and an explosive Playing In The Band solo. Strong segues link China Cat Sunflower into I Know You Rider and the set closes with a wah‑tinged Sugar Magnolia coda. Overall performance is high‑energy despite mixed recording quality across sources.",
    "review": "The March 28, 1972 Academy of Music gig finds the Dead in top form, delivering a concise first set that bursts with classic material and tight transitions. Highlights include a luminous steel‑guitar rendition of Looks Like Rain, a smoking Brokedown Palace, and the unforgettable China Cat Sunflower → I Know You Rider segue where Keith Godchaux’s organ soars alongside Bob Weir’s supernatural lead break. The second set kicks off with a monster Playing In The Band solo that builds to an explosive climax, followed by a wah‑infused Sugar Magnolia coda and a ferocious run through The Other One. While audience tapes from the night are notoriously muddy, the Miller soundboard capture (gd1972-03-28.132729.sbd.miller.flac16) preserves every nuance, making this one of the most compelling snapshots of the 1972 era.",
